<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2025-02-02.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="013855"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013855" label="[01:38:55]">&lt;ztrawhcse&gt;</a></span><span class="message">jackdk: speaking as meson upstream, knowing about hardcoded lists of compilers is not a rule we are unwilling to bend. It is slightly awkward since a lot of the more advanced features that people want from a build system do in fact require knowing compiler-specific details (actual POSIX requirements on a compiler interface are quite sparse), but probably doable. Unfortunately, to date</span></div><div class="line" id="013855"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013855" label="[01:38:55]">&lt;ztrawhcse&gt;</a></span><span class="message">everyone interested in implementing a generic interface has flaked out.</span></div><div class="line" id="015446"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#015446" label="[01:54:46]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Did anyone at meson pinged austingroup (POSIX) about those features? Would make sense to at least standardize some of them.</span></div><div class="line" id="025022"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025022" label="[02:50:22]">&lt;ztrawhcse&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lanodan: at least POSIX issue 8 standardized how to create a shared library :_</span></div><div class="line" id="025649"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025649" label="[02:56:49]">&lt;ztrawhcse&gt;</a></span><span class="message">at any rate, we have officially noted that it seems quite reasonable and we will accept patches for, a) refactoring meson's compiler classes to collect knowledge of &quot;a POSIX compiler&quot; in a single interface, that other (hard-coded) compilers can inherit from, b) using that as a basis for defining a &quot;compiler detected as just-use-POSIX&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="025729"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025729" label="[02:57:29]">&lt;ztrawhcse&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it is quite possible that a &quot;just-use-POSIX&quot; compiler won't necessarily support all meson features, but if you're using a POSIX compiler perhaps you *do not care*</span></div><div class="line" id="025731"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025731" label="[02:57:31]">&lt;ztrawhcse&gt;</a></span><span class="message">like</span></div><div class="line" id="025759"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#025759" label="[02:57:59]">&lt;ztrawhcse&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if you're bootstrapping a system with a POSIX compiler I nearly guarantee that you don't care about using meson's sanitizers option, or enabling meson's LTO option</span></div><div class="line" id="030248"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#030248" label="[03:02:48]">&lt;ztrawhcse&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stuff like checking a get_define or has_function may not be reliable, you'll have to check that out, but not all projects need that anyway...</span></div><div class="line" id="030350"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#030350" label="[03:03:50]">&lt;ztrawhcse&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'd love to see your thoughts on compiler.symbols_have_underscore_prefix(): Returns true if the C symbol mangling is one underscore (_) prefixed to the symbol.</span></div><div class="line" id="030641"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#030641" label="[03:06:41]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, even guix and live-bootstrap bootstrap paths don't really care too much about POSIX</span></div><div class="line" id="030821"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#030821" label="[03:08:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, POSIX is basically a portability standard, so good for some things but when you bootstrap from a very tiny seed, it's simpler to just use all available tools</span></div><div class="line" id="030850"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#030850" label="[03:08:50]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">after all M2-Planet or mescc are also not even C99 compatible...</span></div><div class="line" id="034451"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#034451" label="[03:44:51]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">surely not ansi c compatible either</span></div><div class="line" id="115020"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#115020" label="[11:50:20]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ztrawhcse: Yeah, although one I noticed as missing is -Wl,-soname</span></div><div class="line" id="123536"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#123536" label="[12:35:36]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hi! shortly, <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/pull/493">https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/pull/493</a>  is going to be merged in. this is the beginning of a live-bootstrap distfiles mirror network. if you have a web server with ~2GB free, it would be appreciated if you would consider running a mirror. this is a bit different from a traditional &quot;mirror&quot; as you don't rsync another primary mirror, instead each mirror is</span></div><div class="line" id="123538"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#123538" label="[12:35:38]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">individually collating upstream sources. `mirror.sh` in the PR is responsible for doing that, but you could write your own script too. only dependencies are something to get HTTP files with, and git.</span></div><div class="line" id="123605"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#123605" label="[12:36:05]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's really helpful if the mirror supports plain HTTP as well</span></div><div class="line" id="123620"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#123620" label="[12:36:20]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there is basic instructions in DEVEL.md</span></div><div class="line" id="125439"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#125439" label="[12:54:39]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Wait… you're sure about `git config tar.tar.gz.command gzip` ? Because git internal gzip command is deterministic, gzip(1) isn't</span></div><div class="line" id="130639"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#130639" label="[13:06:39]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Heh &quot;curl: (33) HTTP server does not seem to support byte ranges. Cannot resume.&quot; thanks git.savannah.gnu.org</span></div><div class="line" id="132328"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#132328" label="[13:23:28]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Huh gnulib-8f4538a5.tar.gz curl: (22) The requested URL returned error: 400</span></div><div class="line" id="132342"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#132342" label="[13:23:42]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Which is git.savannah.gnu.org as well…</span></div><div class="line" id="151101"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#151101" label="[15:11:01]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: I'll try to set up a mirror but probably not today</span></div><div class="line" id="191152"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#191152" label="[19:11:52]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; lanodan: when is gzip non-deterministic?</span></div><div class="line" id="191215"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#191215" label="[19:12:15]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; is that across different versions of gzip?</span></div><div class="line" id="191323"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#191323" label="[19:13:23]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, forges have been bitten by this many times</span></div><div class="line" id="191435"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#191435" label="[19:14:35]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm not exactly sure why but pretty sure it's the reason why git got it's own gzip</span></div><div class="line" id="191701"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#191701" label="[19:17:01]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; hmm, maybe it is</span></div><div class="line" id="191706"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#191706" label="[19:17:06]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; I've found some blog</span></div><div class="line" id="191711"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#191711" label="[19:17:11]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; <a rel="nofollow" href="https://medium.com/@mpreziuso/is-gzip-deterministic-26c81bfd0a49">https://medium.com/@mpreziuso/is-gzip-deterministic-26c81bfd0a49</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="191722"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#191722" label="[19:17:22]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; replacing the same commands with bzip2 fixes it though</span></div><div class="line" id="191847"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#191847" label="[19:18:47]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; &quot;gzip -n&quot; seems to work though</span></div><div class="line" id="191911"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#191911" label="[19:19:11]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Nah this blog article just skips a whole lot of problems</span></div><div class="line" id="191954"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#191954" label="[19:19:54]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Effectively because reproducible in the same environment doesn't means it's deterministic across different versions.</span></div><div class="line" id="192041"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#192041" label="[19:20:41]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; yeah, but where else can non-determinism come in?</span></div><div class="line" id="192045"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#192045" label="[19:20:45]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; it's either metadata</span></div><div class="line" id="192055"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#192055" label="[19:20:55]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; or compression algorithm changes</span></div><div class="line" id="192756"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#192756" label="[19:27:56]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Heh well on trying to find it again also leads to re-discovering that the change from gzip(1) to git archive gzip also broke checksums <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/git/git/commit/4f4be00d302bc52d0d9d5a3d4738bb525066c710">https://github.com/git/git/commit/4f4be00d302bc52d0d9d5a3d4738bb525066c710</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="193502"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#193502" label="[19:35:02]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmm, but in principle we don't need to use gzip if we do compression ourselves...</span></div><div class="line" id="193525"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#193525" label="[19:35:25]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">can as well use something more stable, e.g. bzip2</span></div><div class="line" id="224440"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#224440" label="[22:44:40]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lanodan: the reason we do that is because forges generate *their* archives using gzip(1) instead of internal gzip</span></div><div class="line" id="224502"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#224502" label="[22:45:02]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and it is best if the tarballs we generate match upstream snapshots, where they exist</span></div><div class="line" id="224521"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#224521" label="[22:45:21]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in theory, we do not need to do this</span></div><div class="line" id="224533"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#224533" label="[22:45:33]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but practically, until we have more mirrors, i would prefer we do this</span></div><div class="line" id="225024"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225024" label="[22:50:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: so longer term do we plan to stop using upstream snapshots?</span></div><div class="line" id="225118"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#225118" label="[22:51:18]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: as in, use our own snapshots that don't match upstream?</span></div><div class="line" id="225138"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225138" label="[22:51:38]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah... I'm not saying we should do, jus tasking</span></div><div class="line" id="225141"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#225141" label="[22:51:41]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i think we need to discuss that more, i'm not personally opposed to it when we have more people verifying, but i haven't thought much about it</span></div><div class="line" id="225152"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225152" label="[22:51:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ok, let's leave it to future</span></div><div class="line" id="225200"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225200" label="[22:52:00]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I haven't though through it either</span></div><div class="line" id="225409"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225409" label="[22:54:09]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: and can you remind me how mirroring works wrt to live-bootstrap commits?</span></div><div class="line" id="225425"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225425" label="[22:54:25]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we need to rebuild it for each new commit if tarballs change?</span></div><div class="line" id="225754"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225754" label="[22:57:54]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmm, should just look at that PR again</span></div><div class="line" id="225914"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#225914" label="[22:59:14]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: so, ./mirror.sh needs to be rerun on each commit, yes. but it's incremental update; it keeps anything around that still matches checksum</span></div><div class="line" id="225932"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#225932" label="[22:59:32]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also, theres an internal &quot;state&quot; directory that holds git repositories to avoid recloning them</span></div><div class="line" id="225948"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225948" label="[22:59:48]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah ok. Not perfect I guess but at least better than nothing</span></div><div class="line" id="225959"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225959" label="[22:59:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we need to start with something</span></div><div class="line" id="230054"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230054" label="[23:00:54]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's not completely clear how to make older commits work for longer anyway</span></div><div class="line" id="230055"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#230055" label="[23:00:55]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Hmm, i am confused how git archive is currently deterministic lol</span></div><div class="line" id="230121"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#230121" label="[23:01:21]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: well, mirror.sh also doesn't delete old distfiles, so any given mirror should work for any commit mirror.sh is run against</span></div><div class="line" id="230122"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230122" label="[23:01:22]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I guess it's deterministic from 2023?</span></div><div class="line" id="230135"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230135" label="[23:01:35]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh yeah, that's probably helpful</span></div><div class="line" id="230144"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#230144" label="[23:01:44]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sorry, i mean how mirror.sh's invocation is deterministic</span></div><div class="line" id="230153"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#230153" label="[23:01:53]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">because we don't pass -n</span></div><div class="line" id="230155"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#230155" label="[23:01:55]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">is -n default?</span></div><div class="line" id="230157"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230157" label="[23:01:57]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">maybe git archive passes -n</span></div><div class="line" id="230200"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230200" label="[23:02:00]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's not default for gzip</span></div><div class="line" id="230216"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230216" label="[23:02:16]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but maybe if you run gzip on pipes, there is no metadata to store</span></div><div class="line" id="230222"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230222" label="[23:02:22]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as opposed to running on files</span></div><div class="line" id="230242"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#230242" label="[23:02:42]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh - that is probably the case</span></div><div class="line" id="230301"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230301" label="[23:03:01]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, maybe in the future we'll still implement p2p solution for mirroring, then older commits might still be covered by some of the mirrors</span></div><div class="line" id="230316"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230316" label="[23:03:16]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though having http mirror is good too in certain cases</span></div><div class="line" id="230321"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230321" label="[23:03:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">e.g. if you are behind some firewall</span></div><div class="line" id="230346"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#230346" label="[23:03:46]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, i would love for the p2p idea to be implemented some day, but i think this is a reasonable starting point</span></div><div class="line" id="230437"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#230437" label="[23:04:37]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mainly want to increase the robustness of obtaining distfiles currently, which i think this does well</span></div><div class="line" id="230630"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230630" label="[23:06:30]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, and we'll have more copies</span></div><div class="line" id="230637"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230637" label="[23:06:37]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in case some upstreams disappear</span></div><div class="line" id="230646"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230646" label="[23:06:46]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or move urls...</span></div><div class="line" id="230654"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230654" label="[23:06:54]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">e.g. openssl was often breaking</span></div><div class="line" id="230855"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230855" label="[23:08:55]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ok, running mirror.sh now</span></div><div class="line" id="230928"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230928" label="[23:09:28]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">maybe you can test my mirror once it is setup</span></div><div class="line" id="232646"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#232646" label="[23:26:46]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mine is at <a rel="nofollow" href="http://samuelt.me/pub/live-bootstrap/">http://samuelt.me/pub/live-bootstrap/</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="232707"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#232707" label="[23:27:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: <a rel="nofollow" href="http://live-bootstrap.stikonas.eu/">http://live-bootstrap.stikonas.eu/</a>  (https is also there)</span></div><div class="line" id="232719"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#232719" label="[23:27:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though I'm now rerunning my config script to enable autoindex</span></div><div class="line" id="233016"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#233016" label="[23:30:16]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: hmm, mirroring failed...</span></div><div class="line" id="233025"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#233025" label="[23:30:25]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">curl: (6) Could not resolve host: objects.githubusercontent.com</span></div><div class="line" id="233032"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#233032" label="[23:30:32]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this if for <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/rick-masters/lwext4/releases/download/v1.0.0-lb1/lwext4-1.0.0-lb1.tar.gz">https://github.com/rick-masters/lwext4/releases/download/v1.0.0-lb1/lwext4-1.0.0-lb1.tar.gz</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="233034"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#233034" label="[23:30:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think</span></div><div class="line" id="233154"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#233154" label="[23:31:54]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmmmm</span></div><div class="line" id="233218"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#233218" label="[23:32:18]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i can download that file fine, maybe a transient error?</span></div><div class="line" id="233257"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#233257" label="[23:32:57]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, I can download it via the browser at least</span></div><div class="line" id="233353"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#233353" label="[23:33:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but &quot;curl --output lwext4-1.0.0-lb1.tar.gz  <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/rick-masters/lwext4/releases/download/v1.0.0-lb1/lwext4-1.0.0-lb1.tar.gz">https://github.com/rick-masters/lwext4/releases/download/v1.0.0-lb1/lwext4-1.0.0-lb1.tar.gz</a>&quot;  creates zero length  file</span></div><div class="line" id="233424"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#233424" label="[23:34:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">wget works fine on the same host though</span></div><div class="line" id="233454"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#233454" label="[23:34:54]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oooooh</span></div><div class="line" id="233456"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#233456" label="[23:34:56]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">weiiiiird</span></div><div class="line" id="233511"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#233511" label="[23:35:11]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">wait, you need -L for github links</span></div><div class="line" id="233518"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#233518" label="[23:35:18]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">cause they are all 403s</span></div><div class="line" id="233531"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#233531" label="[23:35:31]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">n302*</span></div><div class="line" id="233657"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#233657" label="[23:36:57]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh yes</span></div><div class="line" id="233703"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#233703" label="[23:37:03]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's what you script does</span></div><div class="line" id="235406"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#235406" label="[23:54:06]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: ok, i've tested a few more things (e.g. multiple mirrors),  all seems to work, good to merge?</span></div><br /></div></body></html>